    "result_text": "I added the quick-add bar to the people list, and all 4 Playwright tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works**\n- The bar takes a name and an amount. The server looks the name up the same way the person form compares names: spaces trimmed, case ignored. So `\" dot \"` adds the claim to the existing Dot instead of creating a new person.\n- If the name isn't on the sheet, it adds the person with grade A by calling the existing person service. It then adds a DRAFT claim, dated the sheet's today with an empty note, through the existing claim service. Because the existing services do the work, their checks apply here too, such as duplicate names and refusing claims dated in a closed period. The name and amount have the same limits as the person and claim forms, so a blank name or `-1.00` is refused.\n- Everything runs in one database transaction, and audit records are only written once it commits. If the claim is refused, the new person is rolled back and nothing reaches the audit file.\n- A refusal shows `quick-error` on the bar. The error clears as soon as you edit the bar or submit again.\n\n**Changes**\n- New server code in the `quickadd` package: `QuickAddController` (`POST /api/sheets/{id}/quick-add`) and `QuickAddService`.\n- `PersonRepository` has a new name lookup, `findByName`. I made `PersonView.of` and `ClaimView.of` public so the new controller can build its reply with them.\n- On the front end, `PeopleApi.quickAdd` sends the request, and the bar (`quick-name`, `quick-amount`, `quick-submit`, `quick-error`) is in `people.ts`.\n\nNo database schema change was needed.\n\nThe tests don't cover the case where a brand-new name is quick-added on a day inside a closed period. That's the path where the new person has to be rolled back. I checked it by reading the code, not by running it.",
